---
<p>Bayan has no exceptions: a function that can fail says so in its signature, and the caller must deal with it. This guide runs from declaring an error to cleaning up after one; every example is a complete program.</p>

<h2 id="declare">Declaring errors</h2>
<p>An error set is a <code>union error</code>: each case is one camelCase way to fail on its own line, and its doc comment is the text <code>$"{e}"</code> prints.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    /// The input was empty.
    empty
    /// The input had a non-digit.
    notDigit
}</code></pre>
<p>A function lists the sets it can fail with after its parameters: <code>fails ParseError</code>, or several comma-separated. Public functions must list them; private ones can write a bare <code>fails</code> and let the compiler infer the set.</p>

<h2 id="fail">fail and return</h2>
<p><code>fail .empty</code> leaves the function with an error; <code>return</code> always returns a value, never an error. The case must be in the <code>fails</code> clause, and a function that cannot fail cannot use <code>fail</code>. Both take a suffix:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">fail .empty if s.isEmpty
let c = lookup ?? fail .closed</code></pre>

<h2 id="try-catch">try and catch</h2>
<p>Four ways to handle a failing call:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Written</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>try f()</code></td><td>Passes a failure up to the caller, which must declare the error too.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch value</code></td><td>Uses a fallback value.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch { … }</code></td><td>Runs a block. If <code>f</code> returns a value, the block cannot complete normally: it ends in <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or a panic.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>f() catch switch { … }</code></td><td>Matches on the error; must be exhaustive.</td></tr>
</table>
<example name="errors-basic"/>
<p><code>try</code> covers the whole expression to its right; for a part, add parentheses: <code>(try f()) + g()</code>.</p>

<h2 id="composing">Composing and matching error sets</h2>
<p>A set can be built from others. The result is flat: <code>SignupError</code> below has the cases of both sets, and a component converts to it implicitly. Two components cannot share a case name.</p>
<p>In <code>catch switch</code>, each arm is a pattern that can bind a name: <code>EmailError =&gt; …</code> matches the whole set, <code>DbError.timeout =&gt; …</code> one case, and <code>DbError e =&gt; …</code> binds the error as <code>e</code>.</p>
<example name="errors-switch"/>
<p>Variants belong to their set: <code>EmailError.invalid</code> is not <code>UrlError.invalid</code>, and an ambiguous <code>.invalid</code> is an error.</p>

<h2 id="payloads">Errors with data</h2>
<p>A case can carry values, with payload fields named in the declaration. When you construct it, the label can be left out if there is exactly one payload field; with several, write them as labels or in order.</p>
<p>In <code>catch switch</code>, bind the payload by position with <code>let</code>, or use <code>_</code> to skip a field.</p>
<example name="errors-payload"/>

<h2 id="narrowing">Narrowing a set</h2>
<p><code>except</code> removes cases from a set; <code>catch (Set.variant)</code> handles some and passes the rest on, so the rest must appear in the caller's <code>fails</code>.</p>
<example name="errors-narrow"/>
<p>In <code>retry</code>, <code>rejected</code> becomes the value 1; the other cases leave through <code>Retryable</code>.</p>

<h2 id="cleanup">Cleaning up after a failure</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Written</th><th>Runs</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>defer …</code></td><td>When the scope ends.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>defer failing …</code></td><td>Only if the function fails.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>defer failing (e) …</code></td><td>Only if the function fails, with the error as <code>e</code>.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Deferred statements run in reverse registration order and cannot fail. Only a function that can fail may use <code>defer failing</code>.</p>
<example name="errors-defer"/>
<p>On the failing path, <code>defer 2</code> runs first, then both <code>defer failing</code> statements, then <code>defer 1</code>.</p>

<h2 id="validation">Validating data</h2>
<p>For input that must meet rules, put <code>requires</code> on a type or field. The check runs when a value is constructed, so building one is a call that can fail with <code>ValidationError&lt;T&gt;</code>. Every violated rule is reported, not only the first.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Box {
    i32 w
        requires it &gt; 0
    i32 h
    requires w &lt; h at .w
}

Box build(i32 w, by i32 h) fails ValidationError&lt;Box&gt; =&gt; try Box { w: w, h: h }</code></pre>
<p>The full program is in <a href="features.html#requires">What makes Bayan different</a>. A violation is structured data with a field and a rule, and no message text. A decoder for <code>T</code> uses <code>DecodeError&lt;T&gt;</code>, which joins <code>JsonError</code> and <code>ValidationError&lt;T&gt;</code>; it is planned.</p>
<p>A function precondition differs: write it with <code>expects</code>, and a violation is a bug that panics in every build mode instead of failing.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 discount(i32 total, by i32 percent)
    expects percent in 0..=100
    =&gt; total - total * percent / 100</code></pre>

<h2 id="main">Errors in main</h2>
<p><code>void main() fails ConfigError</code> is allowed. If <code>main</code> fails, the process exits with code 1 and prints the error's doc comment to standard error; <code>@exitCode(n)</code> on a variant chooses another code. Deferred statements still run before the exit.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ConfigError {
    /// The configuration file is missing.
    missing
    /// The configuration is malformed.
    @exitCode(3)
    malformed
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="panics">Panics</h2>
<p>A panic is not an error but a bug: an index out of range, an overflow, a violated <code>expects</code>, division by zero. It prints a message and the source location and aborts, with no unwinding: only <code>defer panicking</code> handlers and <code>Panic.handler</code> run, not plain <code>defer</code> or <code>defer failing</code>.</p>
<p>The spec also describes error return traces in debug builds; they are planned.</p>
